{"assets":["/editorial-assets/d5dc6d0fdf5a7e1880db46cd8d2c1188c0c8ea89f4f8889039f1186cec609d05.png"],"contestId":"54","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d5dc6d0fdf5a7e1880db46cd8d2c1188c0c8ea89f4f8889039f1186cec609d05.png"},"kind":"image"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The most important step on the way to solve this problem - is to understand that it's enough to consider only such rotations of the polygon, that one of its sides lies on the side of the room."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"Let's try to understand this fact. Suppose that the fact is wrong, and there exists such a position of vacuum cleaner, that neither of its sides lies on the side of the room. Denote as "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" the numbers of vertices that lie on the room sides. It's easy to understand that the polygon form between vertices "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" doesn't make any sense itself: for each rotation we can see that from the area of triangle "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"OP"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"P"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] some constant area is subtracted, while the concrete value of this constant depends on the polygon form."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"That's why we see that the polygon form doesn't influence on anything (if we have fixed numbers "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), and we have just to minimize the area of right-angled triangle "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"OP"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"P"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]. But, taking into account that its hypotenuse is a constant, it's easy to see that the minimum is reached in borderline cases: i.e. when one of the polygon sides lies on the room side."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"So, we've proved the fact. Then we have to invent fast enough solution. We have already obtained an "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") solution: iterate over all sides of the polygon (i.e. iterating over all possible "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), mentally push it to one side of the room, then find the threshold point "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then calculate answer for given "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Let's learn how to do these both things in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1)."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"In order to do the first thing (finding "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") we can use a method of moving pointer: if we iterate over "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the same order as in the input file, then we can just maintain the right value of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" (i.e. when we move from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" + 1 we have to increase "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" several times, while it is getting further and further)."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"text","text":"In order to do the second thing (the area calculation) we have to do some precalculation. For example, we can find the mass center "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Q"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" of the vacuum cleaner, and pre-calculate all partial sums "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] - sums of all triangles "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"QP"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1]"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"P"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] for all "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". After this precalculation we can get the answer for every "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1) as a combination of difference of two values from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"S"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"[] and two triangles' areas: "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"QP"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"P"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"] and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"OP"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"P"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"["},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"]."},{"kind":"line_break"},{"kind":"line_break"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/1085"}